Z||000000||Brian84SE||11-02-2000||12:24 PM||||When I went to set my timing (with the diag. terminal grounded like they say to do) the indicator mark on the crank pulley jumps around quite a bit so I almost have to average the oscillations to get a base timing reading. Is this normal or is it a sign that the distributor is worn?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||11-02-2000||08:06 PM||||could be either. I've heard you ground the diag terminal. I've heard you unplug something. Don't know which is right. I don't have a distributor.<P>The ignition module has no dynamic timing. It uses the raw timing set by moving the dist. As I understand it the diag terminal thing is suposed to disable the ECM so only the module fires the ignition. I expect it should be fairly stable unless you don't have a good connection at the ALDL or the dist is messed up some how. (Keep in mind a bad dist cap rotor plug wire or plug can also screw around with the timing light.)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bob Englert||11-02-2000||08:24 PM||||My timing mark also jumps around quite a bit on my 85 SE 4 cyl.  I just try to find the average of where the mark is appearing and go from there.  The car drives quite well so I am not real worried about it.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>87 Coupe<BR>85 SE (For Sale)||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfixit58||11-03-2000||07:19 AM||||Thiming marks that jump around usually mean a worn timing gear or streached timing belt/chain. It can also mean a worn distributor.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||4
